description = "I set up Kubernetes to learn Kubernetes, then packaged the second cluster so it would launch in an afternoon. Three short posts: what the kit costs and what it buys, where k3s genuinely shines against the simpler options, and the one workload I know well enough to prove the edge of it."
+++

I set up Kubernetes to learn Kubernetes. Creating the cluster took an
afternoon; everything after it — delivery, secrets, certificates, tenancy,
observability, backups — took the rest of the week. I did not want to pay for
that week twice, so the second cluster became a repository you fork.

Three posts came out of it.

- **[K3s starter kit on Hetzner cloud](/blog/k3s-platform-kit/)** — what it
  costs (**€122.45 a month**, measured), what it uses before any of your code
  runs (**4,526 Mi**, also measured), and where k3s actually beats the simpler
  answers. Which, below about ten services, is nowhere.

- **[What a game world server actually
  needs](/blog/what-a-game-world-server-needs/)** — a WoW emulator is an old
  favourite of mine and an unusually honest test of a cluster. Almost all of it
  is stateless plumbing that belongs in one. Exactly one process does not, and
  it is the one that is the game.

The platform itself is at
[hcloud-k3s-platform-kit](https://github.com/sasin91/hcloud-k3s-platform-kit):
fork it, edit the values, apply it. Every decision has the reasoning attached,
and the forty-nine things that broke while reporting success are in a file of
their own.

If you have one application, none of this is for you — put it on
[one box](/blog/athletos-freebsd/) for about €7 a month and get on with it. What
flips the decision is rarely the machine count. It is the second person who has
to keep the isolation correct, because that is the point at which "we remember
to do it" stops being true.

Every number in these posts was measured on a cluster that existed. Where I
could not measure something I say so rather than estimating it.
